ABSTRACT Sari, Najwa Kumala. 2016. The Effectiveness of Giving Direct and Indirect Corrective feedback on Eighth Graders’ Recount Compositions. Sarjana’s Thesis, English Department, Faculty of Letters, State University of Malang. Advisors: (I) Dr. Mirjam Anugerahwati, M.A., (II) Prof. Ali Saukah, M.A. Ph.D. Key words: Writing, Direct Corrective Feedback, Indirect Corrective Feedback, Recount Text Writing is important to be mastered by the students. However, despite its importance, writing is not easy to be mastered. Students will find many difficulties when writing so that there is a possibility for them to make errors. This leads to the idea that corrective feedback as a part of writing process. There are two kinds of corrective feedback, direct corrective feedback and indirect corrective feedback. In direct corrective feedback, the teacher provides the correct form while in indirect corrective feedback, the teacher indicates that an error exists but does not provide the correct form. This study is aimed to investigate the significance of the difference between direct and indirect corrective feedback on eighth graders’ recount compositions. This study used quasi-experimental research design. T he samples of this study are chosen by the school. The chosen samples of the study are 20 students of VIII A class and VIII B class of MTs Plus Al-Hadi. There are two groups of the students who are given different treatment. One group is given direct corrective feedback while the other group is given indirect corrective feedback as the treatment. The two groups are given pretest, treatment and posttest. The data were collected through pretest and posttest. The data collected in this study is in the form of students’ writing scores. The result of the posttest of the two groups is compared using statistics in order to draw the conclusion. Based on data analysis using Independent Sample T-test, it was found out that direct corrective feedback can give more significant effect than indirect corrective feedback on students’ recount compositions. It can be concluded that students given direct corrective feedback will achieve better in writing recount text. Based on the result of the study, the future researchers are suggested to include the data on the students’ preferences of the corrective feedback on their writings. Also, they are suggested to investigate further the effectiveness of direct and indirect corrective feedback in different context and with different text types. For the English teachers, they are expected to be able to choose the most appropriate method in giving corrective feedback to the students’ writings particularly in writing recount text. /* Style Definitions */
