ABSTRAK Septiana, Ayu Rizki. 2014. Corrective Feedback on Writing Accuracy as Related to Students’ Different Levels of Grammatical Sensitivity. Graduate Program in English Language Teaching, State University of Malang. Advisors: (I) Dr. Gunadi Harry Sulistyo. M.A. (II) A. Effendi Kadarisman, M.A., Ph.D. Keywords: indirect corrective feedback, direct corrective feedback, errors, writing accuracy, grammatical sensitivity This study investigates whether indirect corrective feedback is effective in improving the students’ writing accuracy and whether there is any interaction between corrective feedback given to the students’ writing and the students’ different levels of grammatical sensitivity. This study adopted a quasi-factorial design with a pretest and posttest group design. The subjects of the study were the forth semester students of English Department, State University of Malang in academic year 2013-2014. Students’ writing accuracy was measured by a writing test in the form of argumentative essay writing test; meanwhile the students’ levels of grammatical sensitivity was measured by an error recognition test adapted from Sharpe (2004) Barron’s TOEFL 11th Edition. The data collected are analyzed using analysis of covariance (ANCOVA). This research reveals the following findings. First, there is no significant difference on the students’ writing accuracy between those who are given indirect corrective feedback and those who are given direct corrective feedback since p-value (.259) was greater than level of significance α = .05. Second, there is significant difference on the students’ writing accuracy between students at high level of grammatical sensitivity who are given indirect corrective feedback and students at high level of grammatical sensitivity who are given direct corrective feedback since p-value (.024) was lower than level of significance α = .05. Third, there is no significant difference on the students’ writing accuracy between students with a low level of grammatical sensitivity who are given indirect corrective feedback and students with a low level of grammatical sensitivity who are given direct corrective feedback since p-value (.686) was greater than level of significance α = .05. Forth, there is no interaction between corrective feedback given to the students’ writing and the students’ different levels of grammatical sensitivity since p-value (.374) was greater than level of significance α = .05. However, it is proven that both of the corrective feedback improves the score of the students’ writing accuracy. Some suggestions are offered in relation to the findings of this research. For the lecturer, it is suggested to respond the students’ writing using indirect corrective feedback since it is proven that indirect corrective feedback improve the students’ accuracy higher than the direct corrective feedback, especially for the students with a high level of grammatical sensitivity. For further researcher, it is suggested to carry out a research related to corrective feedback using different text type and different research setting. Further researchers are also suggested to include content and organization as the component of writing to be given corrective feedback. In addition, it is suggested to add the number of meetings. Normal 0 false false false EN-US X-NONE X-NONE /* Style Definitions */
